{"cut_proposal":{"candidate_errors":[],"prefix":"To solve the problem, we start with the given equation \\(\\cos x + \\sin x = \\frac{1}{2}\\). We will square both sides of this equation to eliminate the trigonometric addition.\n\nFirst, square both sides:\n\\[\n(\\cos x + \\sin x)^2 = \\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)^2\n\\]\nThis expands to:\n\\[\n\\cos^2 x + 2 \\cos x \\sin x + \\sin^2 x = \\frac{1}{4}\n\\]\nUsing the Pythagorean identity \\(\\cos^2 x + \\sin^2 x = 1\\), we substitute:\n\\[\n1 + 2 \\cos x \\sin x = \\frac{1}{4}\n\\]\nSubtract 1 from both sides:\n\\[\n2 \\cos x \\sin x = \\frac{1}{4} - 1\n\\]\nSimplify the right-hand side:\n\\[\n2 \\cos x \\sin x = -\\frac{3}{4}\n\\]\nDivide both sides by 2:\n\\[\n\\cos x \\sin x = -\\frac{3}{8}\n\\]\nWe know that \\(\\cos x \\sin x\\) can be rewritten using the double-angle identity for sine:\n\\[\n\\cos x \\sin x = \\frac{1}{2} \\sin 2x\n\\]\nThus:\n\\[\n\\frac{1}{2} \\sin 2x = -\\frac{3}{8}\n\\]\nMultiply both sides by 2:\n\\[\n\\sin 2x = -\\frac{3}{4}\n\\]\n\nNext, we use the identity \\(\\sin 2x = 2 \\sin x \\cos x\\) to find \\(\\sin x\\) and \\(\\cos x\\). We already have \\(\\sin 2x = -\\frac{3}{4}\\). We also know:\n\\[\n\\cos^2 x + \\sin^2 x = 1\n\\]\nLet \\(\\sin x = a\\) and \\(\\cos x = b\\). Then:\n\\[\na^2 + b^2 = 1\n\\]\nand\n\\[\na + b = \\frac{1}{2}\n\\]\nWe also have:\n\\[\nab = -\\frac{3}{8}\n\\]\n\nWe solve the system of equations:\n1. \\(a + b = \\frac{1}{2}\\)\n2. \\(ab = -\\frac{3}{8}\\)\n\nConsider the quadratic equation whose roots are \\(a\\) and \\(b\\):\n\\[\nt^2 - (a+b)t + ab = 0\n\\]\nSubstitute \\(a + b\\) and \\(ab\\):\n\\[\nt^2 - \\frac{1}{2}t - \\frac{3}{8} = 0\n\\]\n\n","prefix_end":1445,"status":"review_required"},"eligible":false,"generation":{"generation":{"max_new_tokens":2048,"temperature":0.7,"top_k":0,"top_p":1.0},"ids":[1249,11625,279,3491,11,582,1191,448,279,2661,23606,1124,11520,9407,856,488,1124,15940,856,284,1124,37018,90,16,15170,17,11035,568,1205,686,9334,2176,11067,315,